            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house is quite leaky, with 6.32 air changes per hour. During the blower door test, I identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the exterior doors where there may be wear or damage to the weatherstripping



** The current chimney for the propane boiler



** The combustion air duct for your current propane boiler



** Any penetrations through the floor into your crawl space



Conditions were not ideal for infrared camera pictures. However, I also suspect that there is some air leakage at the baseboards of your house, and some in the utility room due to service penetrations. Your planned boiler upgrade will also reduce your air leakage, by eliminating the chimney and the need for the combustion air duct. 



Savings: A 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss is estimated to save $65 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>



The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation, and 1x2 strapping filled with what is likely board insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R15 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding. This would give an R value of approximately R40 in your walls. 



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings: By adding a minimum R15 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $190 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $5,000 in rebates from the DHERP. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



If you are planning on completing a siding retrofit prior to March 31st 2022, please contact me for more information on the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house are generally in good shape. Most are triple pane, wood frame windows, but they do not appear to have any low-E coatings. I do not recommend upgrading the windows at this time, but I have included this upgrade in my report for information purposes. 



I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $190 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.7 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 80% efficient propane boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, as this is what you will be installing next week. You also confirmed that your new system will be a combi system, where the boiler provides both hot water and space heating.



While investigating your current boiler, I noticed that it is connected to your hot water tank. Therefore, I have modelled your current system as a combi system. 



Savings: By replacing your current propane boiler with the 95% AFUE condensing propane boiler you have selected, you could potentially save $1,070 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 36.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 



While updating your boiler you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 5 per fiscal year) from EEIP. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.6 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current propane boiler and your wood stove vent up chimneys and are susceptible to back drafting. However, your new direct vent propane boiler will not be susceptible to back drafting. 



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a range hood   

        - one bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.21 per liter 

Propane - $0.683 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,

Alexandra Giroux, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
